[Music]  hey guys i'm matt welcome back to my  kitchen  a few months ago my friend shreya from  buzzfeed india challenged me to try  maggie noodles for the first time  and then make an original recipe with  them so what i did was take  instant noodles and turn them into an  unnecessarily complicated and  time-consuming sandwich  why has he made a two-minute recipe into  a 20-minute recipe  i've learned a lot since then which is  good because now i have been issued  yet another challenge this time from the  philippines hey matt my name is jessica  i am from manila and today i'm very  excited to introduce to you  a filipino favorite ingredient slash  condiment and that is none other than  the vago ong or the fermented shrimp  paste the one i have here is the regular  ginisang bagong it also comes in  different  flavors so there's sweet there's spicy  and if you can't make up your mind  there's also sweet and spicy  normally we would have this as a dip for  our green mangoes  our boiled vegetables we also add them  in our dishes with pork with stir-fried  vegetables  with other meat we also add them to our  rice as fried rice like bago on fried  rice oh my gosh i'm so hungry so i  challenge you matt  to create your own bhagawang dish using  your creative chef mind and i'm going to  try to remake that here with my very  limited  cooking skills and let's see if my  filipino palette approves  also a word of caution this does smell  really bad especially when you're  cooking it so it's gonna  smell pretty much everywhere i would  apologize to your neighbors if you live  in  like a condo or apartment but it's all  worth it it's all worth it i promise you  already cheska challenge accepted i'm  gonna go and try and find the salty  stinky condiment  [Music]  here it is in all of its glory this is  gina sang  bhagawang there's some oil on the top  there you can see like what i think are  the little shrimp floating inside  let's give it a shot there we go  it's very stinky it's definitely sort of  fishy it's got that ocean smell  it's a little bit anxious it's got it's  got like  i don't know how to describe it's got  like a huh to it let's give it a taste  three two one  wow definitely salty umami my plan is to  make a few dishes use it see how it's  supposed to be used what it's supposed  to pair with  so i went ahead and tried three  different recipes just to sort of see  the range of what you could make and to  gain a better understanding of it first  and foremost i just have some green  mangoes that i'm going to dip in it  then i have a bag on fried rice and then  finally i have this pork belly stew  i have the green unripe mangoes that  looks like a generous helping  this works very well together the mango  is nice and crunchy but that pairs super  super well with the saltiness of the  bhagwon  and now we have the fried rice  it adds like a nice saltiness to it wow  i love that that's my favorite so far  all right now let's try the stewed pork  these two man are so good when you eat  it it warms you all the way down it's  like not  too spicy but it's like just enough that  you're like oh hello you're there this  is going to take a little thought i need  to  meditate a bit see what bagloon would  fit well in and when i come back i'll  kind of have an idea and let's see what  we can whip up  [Music]  welcome back it is day two i had to take  the night to think about things  i liked all the dishes i tried yesterday  but what really got me was how  unique bhagwong is it's so strong it's  so fishy it adds  such a unique flavor to things which  then got me thinking  about anchovies another salty fishy  ingredient that i cook with a lot and  then that got me thinking about putin  esca and so  that's what i think i'm going to  experiment with today making a bagawong  putin esca to get started my pan is  preheating over medium medium high heat  and i'm adding olive oil now i am adding  onions and garlic give that a nice stir  everyone gets nice and coated in the oil  and i'm letting this cook for a few  minutes just until the onions are nice  and softened  that looks excellent now i'm adding some  capers for a little bit of brininess  some red pepper flake for heat and  finally my  baguong i'm gonna go with  one tablespoon  i'm gonna go with two tablespoons that  feels right to me  it's disintegrated and broken up really  nicely into the sauce i'm just gonna let  this cook for a few more minutes and  then check back in  and now that i have this nice umami  spicy fishy  briny oil it is time to add the crushed  tomatoes  stir and let that cook down for a few  minutes  my sauce is looking great my pasta is  cooking for about one minute shy of the  package instructions i just want to give  it a little taste  make sure everything is working  i don't really taste it  call it three okay  oh  now it reads for me everything feels  pretty balanced to me  so i'm just going to add my last two  ingredients my chopped olives  and oregano nice stir  before i get rid of my pasta reserve  like  half a cup of water add my pasta  and i am at capacity and very gently  stir it in and i'm going to add  my pasta water just get everything to  loosen  up the starch in the water is going to  help the sauce cling to the pasta it's  going to help it thicken up a little bit  and there we have it putnesska  here goes nothing see how i did  honestly i'm really pleased with how  this turned out you can taste like the  fishiness from the bug long and adds a  little bit of umami to it it adds a nice  saltiness to it  goes with the capers and olives it's got  a little spice to it i would  probably make this again i'd be more  inclined to make this again because they  sell bagong in giant jars  and they sell anchovies and little teeny  packages unfortunately i am not the  judge of this  i am going to send my recipe back to  cheska to see what she thinks  and hopefully she likes my bhagavan  putnezka  so i just finished uh recreating matt's  recipe  uh on the bagong so i just made this  bago-ong  pasta i was very nervous to make it at  first one because i've never made pasta  in my life  while i was making the sauce i made it a  point to try it every now and then  and i couldn't really taste the pagong  so much  so i added like two more tablespoons  okay  so i guess now is the moment of truth  i'm gonna give my honest feedback  oh good but i would probably add another  bungalow online there  another dollop of boku-on no  she said there's not enough as far as  pastas are concerned this is a pretty  good dish  but if you would tell me that it had  bagoong in there  it didn't feel like it was the star of  the show  if you disagree with how i thought of  this dish please let me know in the  comments below or you can take it to me  personally  on my instagram at franchiseall but  thank you so much matt for giving me  this opportunity to exchange cultures  with you  bye no hard feelings jessica he'll be  around too  hello i'm jumping back on here so i just  had dinner with my family and i just  wanted to do like a follow-up  and some of them actually tasted the  bhagawang  in the dish my brother-in-law found it  uh too salty probably because i added  too much pagong but my father-in-law  thought that it was perfect i guess it's  the preference and what you're expecting  from the dish  so bye matt  oh yes  [Music]

